<h2>Executive Summary</h2>
<p>
This report provides a comprehensive performance review of the retail organization across the three-year period (2021&ndash;2023). Utilizing Microsoft Power BI, raw transaction and master data were cleaned, modeled in a Star Schema, and analyzed through interactive dashboards to answer core business questions regarding revenue, profitability, product mix, salesforce productivity, and budget compliance.
</p>

<h2>Section 1: Answers to Management Questions</h2>

<h3>1. Business Performance</h3>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">1. What is the total revenue?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">Total revenue generated across the three years (2021 to 2023) is <strong>$26 Million</strong>.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">2. What is the total profit?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">The total gross profit generated by the business is <strong>$8 Million</strong>.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">3. What is the overall profit margin?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">The overall profit margin for the organization is <strong>32.52%</strong>, showing healthy underlying profitability.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">4. Is revenue growing year over year?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">Yes, the business demonstrated strong expansion between 2021 and 2022, recording a Year-over-Year (YoY) sales growth of <strong>+76.58%</strong>.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">5. Which year performed best?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>2021</strong> was the highest full-calendar revenue contributor generating approximately <strong>$13.4 Million</strong>, followed closely by <strong>2022</strong> with <strong>$11.8 Million</strong>. <em>(Note: 2023 reflects partial-year / YTD reporting).</em></div>
</div>

<h3>2. Products</h3>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">6. Which are the top 10 products by revenue?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">The top 10 revenue-generating products are: <strong>Product 63, Product 28, Product 47, Product 29, Product 84, Product 59, Product 56, Product 66, Product 33, and Product 81</strong>.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">7. Which are the top 10 products by profit?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">Profit leaders closely match revenue leaders, anchored primarily by <strong>Product 63, Product 28, and Product 47</strong>.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">8. Which products have poor margins?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">Lower-end SKUs such as <strong>Product 79</strong> exhibit margins below 15%&ndash;20%, yielding weak bottom-line contributions despite sales activity.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">9. Which products have the highest demand?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>Product 63 and Product 28</strong> registered the highest unit sales velocity, moving over 250 units each.</div>
</div>

<h3>3. Customers</h3>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">10. Who are the top 10 customers?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">The top 10 customers by purchase value are: <strong>Jesse Hill, Thomas Gonzales, Fred Russell, Philip Bailey, Keith Stephens, Aaron Miller, William Medina, David Mendoza, James Armstrong, and Raymond Alexander</strong>.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">11. What percentage of sales comes from the top customers?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">The top 10 customers account for approximately 7% of total sales, demonstrating high customer diversity and low single-account churn vulnerability.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">12. Which customers generate the highest profit?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>Jesse Hill</strong> and <strong>Thomas Gonzales</strong> generated the highest total profit, aligning directly with their leading order volume.</div>
</div>

<h3>4. Sales Team</h3>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">13. Who is the best-performing salesperson?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>Ryan Welch</strong> is the top-performing salesperson, generating over <strong>$700,000</strong> in revenue.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">14. Who generated the highest profit?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>Ryan Welch</strong> achieved the highest profit contribution, followed by <strong>Scott Mason</strong> and <strong>Roger Ramos</strong>.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">15. Which salesperson has the highest order volume?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>Ryan Welch</strong> handled the greatest number of transactions, closing over <strong>260 orders</strong>.</div>
</div>

<h3>5. Geography</h3>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">16. Which state generates the highest revenue?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>California</strong> is the primary state market, accounting for all recorded retail sales (<strong>$26 Million</strong>).</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">17. Which locations are underperforming?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">Secondary regional outlets with low population densities record low transaction volumes and continuous negative budget variance.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">18. Which locations appear to have growth potential?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">Metropolitan centers such as <strong>Anaheim, Bakersfield, and Fresno</strong> possess high resident counts but relatively low sales penetration, offering immediate expansion upside.</div>
</div>

<h3>6. Budget vs Actual</h3>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">19. Is the organization achieving its budget?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>Yes</strong>, cumulative actual sales exceeded the company's annual budget benchmarks.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">20. Which locations exceeded the budget?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">Major metropolitan stores including <strong>Los Angeles, San Diego, and San Jose</strong> significantly exceeded budgeted expectations.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">21. Which locations missed the budget?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text">Smaller regional stores registered negative variances and fell short of monthly benchmarks.</div>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">22. Which months had the largest budget variance?</span><br>
    <div class="ans-text"><strong>January and February</strong> posted the largest positive budget variance, exceeding monthly targets by more than 30%.</div>
</div>

<h3>7. Strategic Insights</h3>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">23. What are the three biggest strengths of the business?</span>
    <ul>
        <li>Strong profit margin of <strong>32.52%</strong>.</li>
        <li>Healthy, well-diversified customer base with zero single-account dependency.</li>
        <li>Proven flagship products (Product 63 &amp; Product 28) with durable market demand.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">24. What are the three biggest areas of concern?</span>
    <ul>
        <li>Total geographic concentration in California without interstate diversification.</li>
        <li>Sales volume deceleration during 2023 requiring pipeline re-evaluation.</li>
        <li>A wide performance gap between top-quartile and bottom-quartile sales reps.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
<div class="qa-item">
    <span class="q-title">25. What recommendations would you give management?</span>
    <ul>
        <li>Expand sales and distribution into neighboring states (Nevada, Arizona).</li>
        <li>Implement peer sales coaching led by top performers like Ryan Welch.</li>
        <li>Bundle or re-price products with margins below 20% to safeguard gross margin.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<h2>Section 2: Business Insights and Strategy</h2>

<h3>Top 5 Key Findings</h3>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Consistent Profit Engine:</strong> The business converted $26M in sales into $8M gross profit at a reliable 32.52% margin.</li>
    <li><strong>Flagship Product Concentration:</strong> The top 10 products drive ~30% of total revenue, confirming high customer alignment around flagship offerings.</li>
    <li><strong>Sales Team Productivity Spread:</strong> Top salespeople sell more than 3x the volume of lower-quartile reps, demonstrating a clear coaching opportunity.</li>
    <li><strong>High-Population Market Gaps:</strong> Multiple densely populated California cities show disproportionately low sales penetration.</li>
    <li><strong>Low Account Dependency:</strong> The top 10 customers represent only ~7% of sales, eliminating insolvency risk from single-client churn.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Top 5 Business Problems Identified</h3>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Geographic Concentration Risk:</strong> 100% dependency on California exposes revenue to local economic or regulatory shifts.</li>
    <li><strong>2023 Revenue Softening:</strong> Annual order volumes moderated in 2023 compared to peak 2021/2022 performance.</li>
    <li><strong>Margin Drag from Low-Tier SKUs:</strong> Several bottom-tier products have margins below 15%, consuming warehouse footprint for minimal profit.</li>
    <li><strong>Sales Rep Execution Disparity:</strong> Over 25% of sales reps fall significantly below the corporate Average Order Value (AOV) benchmark of $2,360.</li>
    <li><strong>Location Metadata Gaps:</strong> Incomplete geographic tags on occasional sales records limit granular local reporting.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Top 5 Strategic Recommendations</h3>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Interstate Regional Expansion:</strong> Establish distribution partnerships in neighboring growth markets (Nevada, Arizona) to de-risk geographic focus.</li>
    <li><strong>Sales Mentorship &amp; Enablement:</strong> Pair lower-performing reps with Ryan Welch and Scott Mason; focus on upselling higher-margin items.</li>
    <li><strong>SKU Optimization:</strong> Reprice or bundle products with margins below 20% to improve overall product portfolio returns.</li>
    <li><strong>Targeted City Marketing Campaigns:</strong> Allocate local promotional budgets specifically toward high-population, low-penetration cities like Fresno and Bakersfield.</li>
    <li><strong>Quarterly Rolling Budgeting:</strong> Adopt dynamic quarterly forecasts that adjust realistically for seasonality and historical run rates.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Section 3: Technical Architecture &amp; Methodology</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>ETL (Power Query):</strong> Cleaned and appended annual sales files (2021&ndash;2023), enforced consistent typing, removed duplicates, and unified into Fact_Sales.</li>
    <li><strong>Data Model:</strong> Configured a single-direction 1-to-many Star Schema connecting Fact_Sales to Dim_Product, Dim_Location, Dim_Customer, Dim_Salesperson, and Dim_Date.</li>
    <li><strong>DAX Measures:</strong> Developed dynamic measures utilizing CALCULATE, DIVIDE, TOTALYTD, SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR, and RANKX.</li>
    <li><strong>Dashboards:</strong> 7 dedicated pages supporting executive overview, time intelligence, product profiling, customer segmentation, sales team evaluation, geographic analysis, and financial budget tracking.</li>
</ul>

